import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/aspe/aspe3063.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Indicators of Impairment" },
        { id: "s4", label: "THE TWO-STEP TEST – Step One, the Recoverability Screen" },
        { id: "s5", label: "THE TWO-STEP TEST – Step Two, Measuring the Impairment Loss" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Why the Undiscounted Screen Matters" },
        { id: "s7", label: "No Reversal of Impairment Losses" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Asset Groups" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example – Passing the Recoverability Screen" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Worked Example – Failing the Screen and Measuring the Loss" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Relationship to IAS 36" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s16", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">





<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A two-step model borrowed from the US GAAP tradition</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 3063 governs the RECOGNITION and MEASUREMENT of IMPAIRMENT losses FOR long-LIVED tangible AND finite-LIFE intangible ASSETS. GENUINELY distinctively, ASPE applies a TWO-STEP impairment TEST STARTING with an UNDISCOUNTED cash FLOW recoverability SCREEN – CLOSELY mirroring the US GAAP ASC 360 impairment MODEL, RATHER than IAS 36'S direct FAIR-value/value-in-USE comparison (Section 4).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Tangible assets and finite-life intangibles</div><div class="principle">This Section applies TO property, PLANT, and EQUIPMENT, and FINITE-life intangible ASSETS – GOODWILL and INDEFINITE-life intangible ASSETS are TESTED for IMPAIRMENT under a SEPARATE approach WITHIN Section 3064 (see the COMPANION poster).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Indicators of Impairment</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Testing only triggered by evidence of decline</div><div class="text-supporting">IMPAIRMENT testing is TRIGGERED only WHEN events OR changes in CIRCUMSTANCES indicate that an ASSET'S carrying AMOUNT may NOT be recoverable – SUCH as a SIGNIFICANT decrease IN market value, ADVERSE changes IN the asset'S USE or PHYSICAL condition, OR a SIGNIFICANT decline IN the enterprise'S OWN operating PERFORMANCE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>THE TWO-STEP TEST – Step One, the Recoverability Screen</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Comparing carrying amount to undiscounted future cash flows</div><div class="text-supporting">STEP ONE compares the ASSET'S (or asset GROUP'S) carrying AMOUNT to the UNDISCOUNTED sum OF its EXPECTED future NET cash FLOWS – IF the CARRYING amount is LESS than or EQUAL to this UNDISCOUNTED total, NO impairment IS recognized, and the TEST stops HERE, REGARDLESS of the ASSET'S current FAIR value.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>THE TWO-STEP TEST – Step Two, Measuring the Impairment Loss</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Fair value comparison, only if Step One fails</div><div class="text-supporting">ONLY WHERE the CARRYING amount EXCEEDS the UNDISCOUNTED future CASH flows (i.e. STEP one FAILS) does the ENTERPRISE proceed to STEP two – MEASURING the IMPAIRMENT loss as the AMOUNT by WHICH the ASSET'S carrying AMOUNT exceeds ITS fair VALUE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Why the Undiscounted Screen Matters</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Producing fewer impairments than a direct fair value comparison would</div><div class="text-supporting">BECAUSE step ONE uses UNDISCOUNTED cash FLOWS (RATHER than a DISCOUNTED, present-VALUE comparison to FAIR value AS IAS 36 REQUIRES directly), an ASSET can GENUINELY have a LOW current FAIR value WHILE STILL passing the RECOVERABILITY screen – SIMPLY because the SUM of its UNDISCOUNTED future CASH flows exceeds ITS carrying AMOUNT. THIS means ASPE'S two-STEP model can GENUINELY produce FEWER impairments THAN IAS 36 WOULD for the SAME underlying FACTS – a MEANINGFUL, practically SIGNIFICANT divergence, RATHER than a MERELY mechanical ONE.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>No Reversal of Impairment Losses</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Once recognized, an impairment loss is permanent</div><div class="text-supporting">ONCE an IMPAIRMENT loss IS recognized, it IS NOT subsequently REVERSED, EVEN if the CIRCUMSTANCES that CAUSED it LATER improve – CONSISTENT with the US GAAP TRADITION this Section'S TWO-step model DRAWS from, and IN CONTRAST to IAS 36'S own REVERSAL permission FOR assets OTHER than goodwill.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Asset Groups</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Testing at the lowest level of independently identifiable cash flows</div><div class="text-supporting">WHERE an INDIVIDUAL asset does NOT generate CASH flows LARGELY independent OF other ASSETS, IMPAIRMENT is TESTED at the LEVEL of the "ASSET GROUP" – the LOWEST level FOR which IDENTIFIABLE cash FLOWS are LARGELY independent OF the cash FLOWS of OTHER assets and LIABILITIES.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example – Passing the Recoverability Screen</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">No impairment, despite a lower fair value</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: An asset with strong future cash flows but soft fair value</div><div class="wk-body">A piece of specialized equipment has a carrying amount of $900,000. Its expected undiscounted future net cash flows total $1,050,000, but its current fair value (based on market comparables) is only $750,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">SINCE the CARRYING amount ($900,000) is LESS than the UNDISCOUNTED cash FLOWS ($1,050,000), STEP one PASSES – <strong>NO impairment</strong> is RECOGNIZED, DESPITE the ASSET'S fair VALUE ($750,000) being LOWER than ITS carrying AMOUNT. UNDER IAS 36'S direct FAIR-value comparison, THIS same FACT pattern WOULD likely HAVE triggered an IMPAIRMENT loss.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Worked Example – Failing the Screen and Measuring the Loss</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Proceeding to Step Two once the screen fails</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: An asset whose future cash flows have genuinely declined</div><div class="wk-body">A production line has a carrying amount of $1,200,000. Its expected undiscounted future net cash flows total only $950,000, and its fair value is determined to be $800,000.</div><div class="wk-answer">SINCE the CARRYING amount ($1,200,000) EXCEEDS the UNDISCOUNTED cash FLOWS ($950,000), STEP one FAILS, PROCEEDING to STEP two: the IMPAIRMENT loss = $1,200,000 − $800,000 (FAIR value) = <strong>$400,000</strong>, recognized IMMEDIATELY in NET income.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 3063 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Description of the impaired asset or asset group, and the events/circumstances leading to impairment</span><span>Amount of the impairment loss and how fair value was determined</span><span>Segment or line item in which the impaired asset is included</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether indicators of impairment genuinely exist, triggering the test</span><span>Determining the appropriate asset group for testing</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Undiscounted future net cash flows for Step One</span><span>Fair value for Step Two, where the recoverability screen fails</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Relationship to IAS 36</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – ASPE Section 3063 vs IAS 36</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 3063</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 36</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Test structure</span><span>Two-step, undiscounted screen first</span><span>One-step, direct recoverable amount comparison</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Reversal of impairment losses</span><span>NOT permitted</span><span>Permitted, except for goodwill</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">TWO genuinely SIGNIFICANT divergences – the UNDISCOUNTED screening TEST and the NO-reversal rule TOGETHER mean ASPE'S impairment OUTCOMES can DIFFER meaningfully FROM the SAME facts ANALYZED under IAS 36.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every impairment event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Impairment loss recognized</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Permanent – no subsequent reversal permitted</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3061</span><span>Property, Plant and Equipment – the primary asset category this Section's impairment testing applies to</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3064</span><span>Goodwill and Intangible Assets – governs the separate impairment approach for goodwill and indefinite-life intangibles</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s16"><span class="num">16</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Recoverability screen</div><div class="glossary-def">Step One of ASPE's impairment test, comparing an asset's carrying amount to its undiscounted future net cash flows.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Asset group</div><div class="glossary-def">The lowest level for which cash flows are largely independent of other assets and liabilities, used as the basis for impairment testing.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe3063 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
